export const cliSkillFiles: Readonly<Record<string, string>> = {
  'references/ownership.md':
    "# Project and worker ownership\n\nCodex `CODEX_THREAD_ID` locates this agent's private hook-written owner record; `CODEX_SESSION_ID`, when present, must agree. IDs locate credentials, never grant authority. Missing hook records fall back to cwd with a warning; partial or conflicting records fail closed.\n\nClaude and Codex hosts without trustworthy thread lookup require their own bootstrap-provided `--project-owner <own-file>` reference. Keep it private: each worker has its own owner and assignment, never its parent's credential.\n\nGive workers this skill and an absolute checkout or project-set assignment. If startup did not establish that assignment, run `1mcp bootstrap --client codex|claude --event SubagentStart --project <absolute-checkout>` or `--project-set <absolute-definition>` before project-specific calls. Parent cwd and session identity are not assignments. Keep a host-required owner reference on subsequent calls.\n\nWith a project set, `--project <label>` selects members for one call. Select one checkout for checkout-specific tools; make separate calls for single-project tools. Keep the same explicit selection throughout discovery, instructions, inspection, and invocation. Selectors and filters never broaden visibility.\n\nExplicit `--url`, including loopback or forwarding, stays credentialless unless `--project-owner` is explicitly supplied. Local credentials do not authorize remote lifecycle controls.\n\nNew owner records use private temporary storage scoped by OS user and config directory. Hooks and calls must share that temporary directory. Existing records remain until cleanup; do not copy them. OS cleanup or reboot can erase records without releasing runtime ownership. Resolve scoped write-permission failures without dropping ownership or widening permissions to bypass a block.\n",
  'references/preparation.md':
    '# Backend preparation operations\n\nKeep the same runtime (`--url`/`--context`), filters, and target overrides. The assigned session project is the default. Select a checkout for checkout-specific work and preserve `--operation` when supplied.\n\n1. Inspect readiness with `1mcp project prepare inspect <backend>`, even when discovery is empty.\n2. Run `1mcp project prepare start <backend>` only within existing user authorization and runtime permissions. A blocked operation does not authorize enabling automatic preparation or widening permissions.\n3. Monitor an existing or returned job with `1mcp project prepare status <backend> <id>` or bounded `1mcp project prepare wait <backend> <id> --wait-ms 5000`.\n4. After verified readiness, refresh server discovery, read applicable server instructions, inspect the tool schema, and submit a fresh call. Preparation never executes or replays the original tool call.\n\nReport blocked or failed states. Use `1mcp project prepare retry <backend> <id>` only for deliberate authorized recovery, never an automatic retry loop. Cancel with `1mcp project prepare cancel <backend> <id>` only for owned work when appropriate; compatible callers may share the job.\n\nFor installing, adapting, or enabling preparation recipes, use the separately installed `1mcp-preparation-setup` skill. This operational reference does not authorize configuration changes.\n',
  'references/recovery.md':
    '# Release and recovery\n\nRelease only your selected bindings with `1mcp project release`. Workers release their own bindings when finished. Only a verified root SessionEnd permits terminal credential cleanup; Stop, SubagentStop, turn completion, age, and parent termination do not prove worker termination.\n\nPending calls, open proxies, and uncertain leases retain credentials. SessionEnd closes admission, and successful release is persisted before deletion. Inspect uncertain leases and stale locks rather than sweeping by age.\n\nRetry failed terminal cleanup with `1mcp project recover --project-owner <ended-own-file>`, even after checkout removal. Recovery requires a recorded SessionEnd; live owners remain protected.\n\nFor authorized manager removal, use `1mcp project drain` and require `complete=true` before removal. A timeout leaves admission closed until explicit `1mcp project resume`.\n',
  'SKILL.md':
    '---\nname: 1mcp-cli\ndescription: Discover and invoke MCP tools through the 1MCP CLI. Use before operating 1MCP-managed servers, including project selection, backend readiness, and caller lifecycle recovery. Preparation recipe configuration belongs to 1mcp-preparation-setup.\n---\n\n# Use 1MCP tools\n\nRoute upstream MCP capabilities through the 1MCP CLI. Reuse current hook-delivered context for the same assigned target; compact bootstrap output is a server overview, not the server manuals.\n\n## Select the target\n\nNormal local calls omit `--project-owner` and `--project`: use the verified startup or worker assignment and your own caller ownership. A per-call override does not change that default. Retain explicit runtime, filters, and target overrides throughout discovery, instruction loading, schema inspection, and invocation.\n\nRead [project and worker ownership](references/ownership.md) before dispatching workers, switching projects or runtimes, selecting project-set members, or resolving missing ownership. Each worker needs independent ownership and an explicit assignment. Resolve bootstrap coverage gaps before project-specific calls; configuration files do not prove trusted hooks or readiness.\n\n## Discover and invoke\n\n1. If the provider or tool is unknown, use `1mcp inspect --search <query>` or `1mcp inspect <server> --search <query>`. Skip discovery when the target is known.\n2. Read `1mcp instructions <server>` before using that server unless its current instructions for the selected target are already available. Empty instructions and unavailable instructions are different outcomes: resolve reported availability or authorization gaps.\n3. Inspect unknown tool schemas with `1mcp inspect <server>/<tool>`.\n4. Invoke with `1mcp run <server>/<tool> --args \'<json>\'` using the inspected schema.\n\nReuse unchanged tool schemas within a session, including across projects. Refresh project-specific instructions, readiness, authorization, and target selection when they change; refresh discovery/schema after preparation or relevant failures. Never automatically replay failed calls.\n\nSearch is case-insensitive literal substring matching. Use quoted `--search "filesystem/*read?" --glob` for whole-reference matching with `*` and `?`. `--include-descriptions` broadens search to descriptions; `--show-descriptions` only displays them. Narrow the server set with `--preset`, `--tags`, or `--tag-filter` when appropriate.\n\nFor a loading static server (`type: external`), use `1mcp wait <server>`. For backend preparation requirements, including an unavailable dynamic server (`type: template`), read [preparation operations](references/preparation.md). Authenticate only with an authorized credential for the selected runtime; consult `1mcp auth login --help` rather than changing targets to bypass a denial.\n\n## Finish or recover\n\nRead [release and recovery](references/recovery.md) before releasing owned bindings, recovering terminal cleanup, or draining a project. Workers explicitly release their own bindings when finished. Turn completion is not SessionEnd and does not authorize deleting owner records.\n\nUse a private task directory under the OS temporary directory for scratch files and payloads. Let 1MCP manage owner records; resolve scoped permission failures rather than copying credentials or bypassing ownership.\n',
};
